1236		Let $G = (U, E)$ be an undirected graph with vertex set $U$ and nonempty edge set $E$. Edges in $E$ are written as multisets $\{u, v\}$ of two vertices; the case $u = v$ represents a self-loop. Suppose there are $m \ge 1$ edges, $k$ of which are self-loops. Then the \emph{graph distribution $\mu_G$ of $G$} is the distribution over $U \times U$ such that for every $(u,v) \in U \times U$,
1237		\[
1238			\mu_G(u, v) =
1239			\begin{cases}
1240				1/(2m-k) & \text{if } \{u,v\} \in E\,,\\
1241				0 & \text{otherwise}\,.
1242			\end{cases}
1243		\]
1244		This is identical to the uniform distribution over ordered pairs $(u,v) \in U \times U$ such that $\{u,v\} \in E$: each edge that is not a self-loop contributes two ordered pairs, and each self-loop contributes one, whence the normalization $2m-k$.
1245	\end{definition}
